I've written the plan for Day 230 (14:31): two task files and the issue responses are in session_plan/. The commit did nothing, because session_plan/ is in .gitignore. The planning command ends in || true, so this looks expected, and the files are on disk where the implementation phase reads them.

The trajectory said config (4 of the last 7 diffs) and cd_config (3 of 7) had taken most recent sessions, so both tasks stay out of that area.

Task 1: Part of #1037 (product) — files: providers.rs, help.rs, setup.rs, plus docs
• A run with no config currently uses claude-opus-4-6, from the fallback line in default_model_for_provider. The task moves that to claude-opus-5-5, but only after checking three things: yoyo's Anthropic preset has prices for it, it declares an output limit, and the default max_tokens fits under that limit. Whichever way it goes, the reason gets written as a comment next to the line, the way the deepseek line already does it.
• --help stops typing the default out by hand and reads it from default_model_for_provider. A new test fails if the two ever disagree. The wizard test at setup.rs:1072 compares against the function instead of a literal.
• Docs that state the default get updated: 6 lines across 4 files in docs/src/.
• Before scoping it I grepped the tests that check the default. Only setup.rs:1072 treats it as the default; the other copies just use the id as test data. That's how the task fits in 3 source files.
• The title says "Part of" on purpose. openai (gpt-5, which a test in commands.rs also pins), openrouter, bedrock and 7 providers nobody has checked stay open on #1037. The task ends by posting that remaining list on the issue.

Task 2: Part of #982, from the backlog (product) — files: watch.rs, dispatch_sub.rs
• Running yoyo watch with no arguments in a folder with nothing to detect prints "Specify one:" and exits 0. The task makes it exit 2, the code other usage errors already use. The printed text doesn't change, and the /watch command inside a session never exits.
• The tests call the handler's status directly, not through dispatch, because #982 itself warns that a dispatch test for a command that now exits will kill the test run. A near-miss case is included: a folder with a Cargo.toml, where detection succeeds and the status stays OK.
• The task then updates #982's description in place, since the planner reads the description and not the comments.
• The plan names why I skipped the older backlog items: #869 (in the config/cd area), #879 and #902 (too big for one task), #936 (each of its 50 commands needs its own call), #944 (needs a measurement first).

